  "findings": [
    "The current public September-2026 grid and adopted programme appendix agree on ten six-credit first-year subjects, ten second-year subjects and thirty open third-year credits followed by Hybrid Intelligence, Computational Linguistics, Ethics of AI and a twelve-credit thesis. The adopted table explicitly labels Years 2/3 as 2027–2028/2028–2029. Hybrid Intelligence replaces old Knowledge Representation. Zero-credit GROW remains separate.",
    "The public mobility window offers four options or a mix. Retained first-listed Exchange/Study Abroad by the neutral official-order tie-break, independent of UCR fit. EER 3.7 permits Board-approved partner-university study subject to academic quality, coherence, non-overlap, admission and safety conditions. No host, package, internship or optional CSAI elective is invented or added. The thirty-credit space is genuinely open, not an uninstantiated restricted subject list.",
    "The appendix requires Statistics I and one earlier programming course for Machine Learning; one earlier programming course for Advanced Programming; Critical and Scientific Thinking, Statistics I and ten participant-pool hours for Research Workshop. The ordinary curriculum completes these first. Deep Learning assumes calculus, linear algebra, Python and machine learning; the earlier scheduled subjects supply them. Completed propaedeutic courses plus sixty postpropaedeutic EC and semester-six-or-later entry govern the thesis. Completing both first years supplies the credits and preparation.",
    "Twenty-four dated 2026 catalogue entries were retrieved and inspected for content, credit values and applicable entry conditions, alongside the adopted future cohort table. Research Workshop designs and reports a group experiment or a machine-learning study. The thesis is supervised empirical quantitative CSAI research, with code, analysis, oral reporting and an academic report. An old 2025–2026 thesis manual was a lead only; its content is not used as authority for current rules.",
    "Course content governs comparison: AI1 combines search and uncertainty with history/philosophy; AI2 surveys learning, networks, language, vision, reinforcement learning and robotics. HCI teaches task analysis, interface design and evaluation; Multi-Agent Systems centres agent-based modelling, simulation, verification and validation. Cognitive Neuroscience investigates functions through neural methods and experiments. Language/Cognition/Computation studies human acquisition and processing, whereas Computational Linguistics implements tokenisation, tagging, parsing and semantic models. Hybrid Intelligence combines knowledge-based recurrent networks, symbolic explanations and human-in-the-loop decisions. These distinctions reject several original generic placements."
